WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.620
Hey, welcome to Models and Agents, episode 76 for June 10th, 2026.

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:09.060
Let's see what happened in the AI world today, and trust me, it's been busy.

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:14.460
Cloud Fable 5 delivers a qualitative jump for long horizon agentic work,

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:19.200
letting builders hand off ambitious multi-step projects with less oversight.

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:25.680
Anthropic released Cloud Fable 5, the same base model as Mythos, but with added safeguards.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:31.800
Early testers reported handles ambitious, long-running problem solving sessions far better than

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:37.080
prior versions, executing complex tasks across code bases without constant guidance.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:43.480
The model posts leading benchmark numbers and feels like a step change comparable to the Cloud 4.5

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:50.260
jump last November. Builders can now attempt larger single-use apps, custom dashboards or research

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:57.020
projects that previously required heavy scaffolding. The main caveats noted are occasional over-triggering

00:00:57.020 --> 00:01:03.220
safeguards and the usual slow, expensive profile of frontier models. This update continues the

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:08.740
frontier model's arc we tracked yesterday, where closed systems trade capability and price leads,

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:14.920
while the next release cadence remains the key open question. Coher open-sourced North Mini-code,

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:23.280
a 30 billion parameter model with 3 billion active parameters under Apache 2.0. It scores 33.4 on the

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:29.340
artificial analysis coding index, competitive with similar-sized models for agentic coding tasks.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:34.540
The release includes hugging face weights for immediate local or fine-tuned use.

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:40.440
Builders working on coding agents should test it this week against closed models on multi-file

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:46.800
edit and tool-use benchmarks to see where the efficiency trade-off lands. This release fits the

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:52.700
open-weight models program we covered yesterday, where families like Lama, Mistrel, and Quenn

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:58.460
continue narrowing the gap with closed frontier systems, and licensing terms stay the central open

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:06.120
question. Next end to Mini is a new 35 billion model explicitly optimized for autonomous agent

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:11.960
workloads. The release positions it as a dedicated agent backbone rather than a general

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:18.740
purpose model. Early positioning suggests stronger, long horizon planning and tool orchestration

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:24.820
that dense models of similar size. Developers building production agents should compare it against

00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:33.080
Quenn and Lama variants on agent-specific harnesses. Users are running Quenn 3.6 MTP 27 billion

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:42.940
quantized a 4-bit on a Tesla V100 via Lama dot CPP. The run reaches 44 to 55 tokens per second.

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:49.100
Flag such as spec-type draft MTP plus large context are active during the test.

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:57.520
The configuration supports 262,000 tokens of context. Parallel batching stays enabled with no quality

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:03.340
loss recorded in non-thinking mode. Local developers now have a concrete performance baseline for

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:10.780
27 billion class models on older enterprise GPUs. Microsoft updated Co-Pilot Studio with improved

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:16.620
agent orchestration and workflow tooling. The changes target more reliable, multi-step automation

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:23.840
inside Microsoft 365 environments. Teams already using Co-Pilot can now build agents that

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:30.300
handle longer task chains with better state management. The upgrade lowers the barrier for enterprises

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:36.120
wanting governed agent deployments without custom frameworks. This development advances the agents

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:42.740
and tool use program we followed yesterday where autonomous agents, tool use and interoperability layers

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:50.160
are maturing and long horizon reliability remains the key open question. TabClaw is an open-source agent

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:57.060
that ingests CSV or Excel files, clarifies intent, exposes editable plans and runs parallel

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:03.720
specialist agents across multiple tables. It records workflows, extracts user memory and distills

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:10.240
reusable skills from repeated patterns. The system improves task completion on spreadsheet benchmarks

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:16.400
while keeping the full execution trace inspectable. Teams doing data analysis should try the react

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:22.800
style loop on their own multi-table data sets. A global financial watchdog issued new guidance on

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:28.720
controlling autonomous AI agents in finance. The guidance addresses prevention of data leakage during

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:34.800
live operations. Unauthorized actions in trading or customer systems form another core focus.

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:40.600
Institutions running agentic workflows receive a direct recommendation to review guard rails.

00:04:41.320 --> 00:04:46.640
The proposed standards serve as the reference point for that review. Deployment decisions should

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:52.100
incorporate the new expectations before any production rollout. This item tracks the safety

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:57.940
and policy program we covered yesterday where US and EU regulatory trajectories and evaluation

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:05.900
standardization remain the open questions. Hugging face relaunched papers with code.co with automatic

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:13.460
parsing of archive and hugging face papers into leaderboards including closed models like GPT 5.5 and

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:20.200
mythos 5. Users can toggle closed model results and browse scatter plots plus tables for each benchmark.

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:24.780
The site now treats blog posts as valid sources for closed models.

00:05:25.540 --> 00:05:30.400
Researchers tracking state-of-the-art across domains should added to their weekly review workflow.

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:37.000
A new tutorial shows how to stream NVIDIA Nemotron code pre-training metadata,

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:44.520
reconstruct GitHub URL's s, fetch source files, and estimate token counts without full data set downloads.

00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:51.100
Schema inspection forms the first step in the described process. Language distribution across

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:58.360
the metadata receives explicit coverage. Token scale estimation relies on pandas combined with Tiktokin.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:04.900
Developers building custom code pre-training pipelines can follow the exact steps for manageable sampling.

00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:10.620
Okay, let's pop the hood on bi-temporal memory engines for LLM agents.

00:06:11.560 --> 00:06:16.800
Everyone talks about giving agents full history as the safety fault for long running tasks,

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:22.840
yet replaying every prior turn quickly turns context into noise that hurts accuracy while

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:29.580
exploding cost and latency. Ingram splits the problem into a fast, lossless right path that

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:35.940
simply appends episodes and an asynchronous path that extracts atomic facts into a bi-temporal

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:41.480
knowledge graph. The graph tracks provenance and supersession chains, so contradictions are

00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:48.040
invalidated rather than deleted. At read time, a hybrid retriever fuses dense, lexical, graph,

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:55.320
and recency signals then applies a point-in-time filter to return a compact 9,600 token slice.

00:06:56.440 --> 00:07:07.180
On long mem eval underscore s, this lean slice scores 83.6% versus 73.2% for the full 79,000 token

00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:14.360
history while using roughly one eighth the tokens. The practical takeaway is to default to retrieved

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:20.520
time-aware facts for any agent that spans more than a handful of turns and only fall back to full

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:27.200
replay when the task explicitly requires verbatim earlier dialogue. Test Cloud Fable 5 on a

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:32.940
multi-file refactoring task you previously split across several prompts and note where it maintains

00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:39.320
context without extra scaffolding. Download Coal here in North Mini Code from Hugging Face and run

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:44.700
it through your existing agentic coding harness to measure tool-called reliability versus closed

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:51.440
models. Try tabclaw on a three-table financial reconciliation spreadsheet to see how the parallel

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:59.000
specialist agents and editable plan surface compare to manual pandas work. Add papers with code.co

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:05.200
to your weekly state of the art check and toggle closed models off to focus on open weight progress.

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:11.320
Further tuning of Cloud Fable 5 safeguard sensitivity is expected in the coming weeks.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:17.020
Additional open agentic coding models from other labs are likely to follow Cohears release.

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:23.720
Expanded regulatory guidance on agentic systems in finance and data handling domains is on the way.

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:30.980
New long context memory benchmarks that stress bi-temporal retrieval over full history baselines are

00:08:30.980 --> 00:08:37.240
